A1. When love comes in the door this flies out the window. ~ FRIENDSHIP
A10. This is found in the middle of the ocean. ~ SALT
A14. At this time of the year King Solomon had to buy a thousand hats. ~ EASTER
A15. Likewise. ~ ALSO
A17. Once more the villain speaks. ~ HAH
A18. A sailor's affirmative. ~ AY
A19. A small matter. ~ ATOM
A21. Praise. ~ LAUD
A22. Note of the scale. ~ MI
A24. What all movie heroes cling to in a shipwreck. ~ SPAR
A26. Shave-tail (abbr.). ~ LT
A27. By means of. ~ PER
A28. A pre-Volstead beverage. ~ TEA
A30. This is hot stuff. ~ STEAM
A32. In the gold-digger's lexicon there's no such word. (Contraction). ~ CANT
A33. Nowadays when women do this it's a takeoff from start to finish. ~ DRESS
A35. Where bad Englishmen go. ~ ELL
A36. This is a sticker. ~ PASTE
A37. Something chorus girls love to do. ~ EAT
A38. Correlative of "either." ~ OR
A40. Something people beg for. ~ ALMS
A41. Languid Yaks (init.). ~ LY
A42. A primitive good luck symbol (pl.). ~ SWASTIKAS
A43. The printer's half measure. ~ EN
A44. Past participle of the verb "be." ~ BEEN
A45. Where married men stay in the evening. ~ IN
A46. One of these in the hand is worth two on the head. ~ BAT
A47. This used to come in kegs at Christmas time. ~ CHEER
A49. Rabble. ~ MOB
A51. An angry Italian's cutting reply. ~ SLASH
A53. What laundry men do whenever possible. ~ TEAR
A54. Men walk with these on Fifth avenue. ~ CANES
A56. Something very few stars do. ~ ACT
A57. Weapon. ~ ARM
A58. An exclamation. ~ AH
A59. Satisfy. ~ SATE
A62. Best Man (init.). ~ BM
A63. Something both love and liquor make the whole world do. ~ SPIN
A65. Eating sirloin steak this way is not done. ~ RARE
A67. What doers do. ~ DO
A68. The brown derby. ~ LID
A70. Greek God of Love. ~ EROS
A72. This is always casting reflections on pretty girls. ~ MIRROR
A74. Something artists do. ~ ETCH
A75. Platform of all political parties. ~ PROSPERITY

D1. A notable achievement like getting the right telephone number. ~ FEAT
D2. A beam. ~ RAY
D3. Part of the verb "be." ~ IS
D4. Greek letter. ~ ETA
D5. These control the wild waves. ~ NETS
D6. Aviation poison; it doesn't take many of these to kill. ~ DROPS
D7. Exclamation. ~ HA
D8. A famous wind. ~ ILL
D9. Sacred song. ~ PSALM
D11. This is number seven backwards. ~ AH
D12. What a bachelor's friends do when he gets married. ~ LAMENT
D13. An unlucky child. (Ask the man who owns one.) ~ THIRTEENTH
D16. Where cats go at night. ~ OUT
D20. What a man gets when he marries. (Trouble has seven letters.) ~ MATE
D23. What women do when they encounter one another. ~ MEET
D25. Things that husbands don't like about their wives. ~ RELATIONS
D27. School-teachers' motto is "They shall not—" ~ PASS
D28. Something a waiter waits with. ~ TRAY
D29. Like. ~ AS
D31. You know me! ~ AL
D32. Projecting parts of a wheel. ~ CAMS
D33. Kind of a girl men don't forget. ~ DELECTABLE
D34. One who sows oats. ~ SOWER
D36. What a married man does when he plans to have a night out. ~ PLANS
D39. Something Mayor Hylan also did last election. ~ RAN
D40. Amalgamated Kleptomaniacs Institute (init.). ~ AKI
D42. A prognosticator. ~ SEER
D43. Section of the U. S. A. owned by Al Smith. ~ EAST
D44. Old Sol's smile. ~ BEAM
D46. Where the Prince of Wales went after his visit to America. ~ BACK
D48. A fellow who enjoys his own company. ~ HERMIT
D49. A well-known governess. ~ MA
D50. A bull's enemy. (Financy that!) ~ BEAR
D52. Southern State (abbr.). ~ LA
D54. Bird talk. ~ CHIRP
D55. This sticks to the job when it's licked. ~ STAMP
D58. A tree dweller. ~ APE
D60. A great lake. ~ ERIE
D61. A small boat. ~ DORY
D64. A negative particle. ~ NOR
D66. What ball players do when they make errors. ~ ERR
D67. This goes with a dash. ~ DOT
D69. Playground for Congressman (abbr.). ~ DC
D71. In a like manner. ~ SO
